# Real-time Multiplayer WebSocket Game Server on Cloudflare Workers (2026)

**Scope note:** All snippets below are drawn from official sources fetched on 2026-07-25. Primary sources are `developers.cloudflare.com` (Durable Objects, Workers, Wrangler, Static Assets, Testing), the `hono.dev` docs, and `github.com/cloudflare/workers-sdk`. Where I synthesize a Hono-specific line from a Cloudflare-documented pattern, I label it. The two repo HEAD SHAs I resolved for permalink construction: `cloudflare/workers-sdk@main` = `6e0bf6e917bf4a2b9cd3ee741e625174075e38e1`; `honojs/hono@main` = `26d8e42bfff7eb78fc323a01e1d02d63b9dbcc72`.

**Deprecated/flagged up front:**
- The `migrations` array (with `new_sqlite_classes`/`new_classes`) is now the **legacy** flow. Cloudflare introduced a declarative `exports` field that replaces it; both are supported but mutually exclusive. ([Durable Object class exports](https://developers.cloudflare.com/durable-objects/reference/durable-objects-migrations/), last updated 2026-07-15)
- `new_classes` (key-value storage backend) **cannot create new namespaces** except for accounts that already have a KV-backed namespace. The **Workers Free plan only supports SQLite-backed DOs** (error `free_tier_requires_sqlite`). So `new_sqlite_classes` is the required key on the Free plan. (Same page, "Storage backends" / "Error reference".)
- KV-backed Durable Object Storage is labeled **Legacy** in the docs sidebar. ([KV-backed Durable Object Storage (Legacy)](https://developers.cloudflare.com/durable-objects/api/legacy-kv-storage-api/))

---

## 1. Durable Objects WebSocket Hibernation API

### Why hibernation matters for cost
The Hibernation WebSocket API lets a Durable Object be evicted from memory while keeping its WebSocket clients connected. **Billable Duration (GB-s) charges do not accrue during hibernation**; when a message arrives the DO wakes and its constructor re-runs. ([Use WebSockets — "Why use Hibernation?"](https://developers.cloudflare.com/durable-objects/best-practices/websockets/#why-use-hibernation), page last updated 2026-06-19.)

### Exact class shape and handlers
- Base class: `import { DurableObject } from "cloudflare:workers"`; `class X extends DurableObject` (TS: `extends DurableObject<Env>`). The state lives on `this.ctx` (a `DurableObjectState`). ([Durable Object Base Class](https://developers.cloudflare.com/durable-objects/api/base/), last updated 2026-06-15; [Durable Object State](https://developers.cloudflare.com/durable-objects/api/state/), last updated 2026-06-03.)
- `this.ctx.acceptWebSocket(ws)` — accepts the server side and makes the DO "hibernatable." Signature: `acceptWebSocket(ws: WebSocket, tags?: Array<string>)`. Tags are max 256 chars each, at most 10 tags per socket, and are retrievable via `getWebSockets(tag)`. Max **32,768 WebSocket connections per DO**. ([acceptWebSocket](https://developers.cloudflare.com/durable-objects/api/state/#acceptwebsocket), [getWebSockets](https://developers.cloudflare.com/durable-objects/api/state/#getwebsockets).)
- Handler methods on the DO class (all optional, all may be `async`):
  - `webSocketMessage(ws: WebSocket, message: string | ArrayBuffer)` — not called for control frames; the runtime auto-answers RFC 6455 pings without waking the DO.
  - `webSocketClose(ws: WebSocket, code: number, reason: string, wasClean: boolean)`.
  - `webSocketError(ws: WebSocket, error: unknown)`. ([Base class handler reference](https://developers.cloudflare.com/durable-objects/api/base/#websocketmessage).)
- `this.ctx.getWebSockets(tag?: string): WebSocket[]` — attached sockets, optionally filtered by tag.
- Serializable attachments (per-connection state that survives hibernation): `ws.serializeAttachment(value: any): void` and `ws.deserializeAttachment(): any`. Persist through hibernation while the socket is healthy; **max serialized size 16,384 bytes**; must be structured-cloneable. ([serializeAttachment](https://developers.cloudflare.com/durable-objects/best-practices/websockets/#websocketserializeattachment).)
- Related: `this.ctx.setWebSocketAutoResponse(new WebSocketRequestResponsePair("ping","pong"))` for app-level ping/pong that doesn't wake hibernated sockets. ([setWebSocketAutoResponse](https://developers.cloudflare.com/durable-objects/api/state/#setwebsocketautoresponse).)
- Compat flag `web_socket_auto_reply_to_close` is **on by default for `compatibility_date >= 2026-04-07`**: the runtime auto-completes the close handshake, so `ws.close()` inside `webSocketClose` is safe but no longer required. ([webSocketClose notes](https://developers.cloudflare.com/durable-objects/api/base/#websocketclose).)
- Local-dev caveat: before `wrangler@3.13.2` / Miniflare `v3.20231016.0`, WebSockets did **not** hibernate under `wrangler dev` (events still delivered; DO never evicted). ([Use WebSockets — "Local development support"](https://developers.cloudflare.com/durable-objects/best-practices/websockets/).)

### Minimal TypeScript example (verified shape)
Adapted from the official hibernation example ([source](https://developers.cloudflare.com/durable-objects/examples/websocket-hibernation-server/), last updated 2026-04-21) and the [Use WebSockets](https://developers.cloudflare.com/durable-objects/best-practices/websockets/#hibernation-example) snippet:

```ts
import { DurableObject } from "cloudflare:workers";

export interface Env {
  ROOMS: DurableObjectNamespace<Room>;
}

export class Room extends DurableObject<Env> {
  async fetch(request: Request): Promise<Response> {
    const pair = new WebSocketPair();
    const [client, server] = Object.values(pair);

    // acceptWebSocket makes this socket hibernatable.
    // Tags are optional; you can later filter with getWebSockets("player").
    this.ctx.acceptWebSocket(server, ["player"]);

    // Per-connection state that survives hibernation (<= 16,384 bytes).
    server.serializeAttachment({ joinedAt: Date.now() });

    return new Response(null, { status: 101, webSocket: client });
  }

  async webSocketMessage(ws: WebSocket, message: ArrayBuffer | string) {
    const state = ws.deserializeAttachment() as { joinedAt: number } | null;
    // Broadcast to everyone in this DO instance:
    for (const peer of this.ctx.getWebSockets()) {
      peer.send(`[${state?.joinedAt ?? 0}] ${message}`);
    }
  }

  async webSocketClose(ws: WebSocket, code: number, reason: string, wasClean: boolean) {
    // On compat_date >= 2026-04-07 the runtime already completed the close handshake.
    ws.close(code, reason);
  }

  async webSocketError(ws: WebSocket, error: unknown) {
    console.error("ws error", error);
  }
}
```

(The `Env` interface and `DurableObjectNamespace<Room>` typing pattern is the one used across the Cloudflare DO examples and the Hono DO example — see §3.)

