## 3. Routing WebSocket upgrades through Hono to a Durable Object

The authoritative Hono + DO pattern is from Hono's ["Cloudflare Durable Objects" example](https://hono.dev/examples/cloudflare-durable-objects) (page last updated 2026-07-23), which uses a Hono app as the Worker's `fetch` handler and obtains a DO stub via `c.env.<BINDING>.idFromName(...).get(...)`. The Cloudflare-side WebSocket upgrade-detection and `stub.fetch(request)` forwarding pattern is from the [websocket-hibernation-server example](https://developers.cloudflare.com/durable-objects/examples/websocket-hibernation-server/) Worker handler.

```ts
import { Hono } from "hono";
import { Room } from "./room"; // your DurableObject class

type Bindings = {
  ROOMS: DurableObjectNamespace<Room>;
  ASSETS: Fetcher;
};

const app = new Hono<{ Bindings: Bindings }>();

// WebSocket upgrade endpoint.
app.get("/ws/:roomId", async (c) => {
  // 1. Detect the Upgrade: websocket header.
  const upgrade = c.req.header("Upgrade");
  if (upgrade !== "websocket") {
    return c.body("Expected Upgrade: websocket", 426);
  }

  // 2. Pick the DO instance by name (one Room per roomId).
  const roomId = c.req.param("roomId");
  const id = c.env.ROOMS.idFromName(roomId);
  const stub = c.env.ROOMS.get(id);

  // 3. Forward the original Request to the DO; it returns the 101 + client socket.
  //    `c.req.raw` is Hono's accessor for the underlying Request.
  return stub.fetch(c.req.raw);
});

// Everything else falls through to static assets.
app.all("*", (c) => c.env.ASSETS.fetch(c.req.raw));

export default app;
```

Notes:
- The `c.env.ROOMS.idFromName(...)` / `c.env.ROOMS.get(id)` and `export default app` pattern is verbatim from the [Hono DO example](https://hono.dev/examples/cloudflare-durable-objects). Hono also recommends generating binding types with `wrangler types --env-interface CloudflareBindings` and passing them as `new Hono<{ Bindings: CloudflareBindings }>()`. ([Hono — Cloudflare Workers: Bindings](https://hono.dev/docs/getting-started/cloudflare-workers#bindings).)
- The `Upgrade: websocket` check and `return stub.fetch(request)` forwarding is the documented Cloudflare Worker pattern ([websocket-hibernation-server Worker handler](https://developers.cloudflare.com/durable-objects/examples/websocket-hibernation-server/)). The only Hono-specific adaptation is using `c.req.raw` to pass the original `Request`; this is standard Hono API but the exact line is a synthesis of the two sources, not a single fetched snippet.
- Cloudflare recommends validating the upgrade in the Worker (not the DO) because both Workers and DOs bill per request. ([Use WebSockets — "Validate requests in a Worker"](https://developers.cloudflare.com/durable-objects/best-practices/websockets/#websocket-standard-api).)

