## 5. Durable Object alarms for server tick loops

Source: [Alarms](https://developers.cloudflare.com/durable-objects/api/alarms/) (last updated 2026-04-21), plus the [alarm handler](https://developers.cloudflare.com/durable-objects/api/base/#alarm) reference and [Limits](https://developers.cloudflare.com/durable-objects/platform/limits/) (last updated 2026-06-01).

- **Storage methods** (on `this.ctx.storage`):
  - `setAlarm(scheduledTimeMs: number): void` — schedule the DO to wake at a UNIX-epoch ms timestamp. Calling it again overrides the existing alarm.
  - `getAlarm(): number | null` — returns the current alarm time or `null`.
  - `deleteAlarm(): void` — cancel.
- **Handler:** `async alarm(alarmInfo?: { retryCount: number; isRetry: boolean })`. Only one `alarm()` runs at a time per DO instance. Guaranteed **at-least-once**; on uncaught exception it retries with **exponential backoff starting at 2 s, up to 6 retries**. (Same page, "Background" / "alarm".)
- **One alarm per DO at a time.** To run a tick loop, store upcoming events in storage and have `alarm()` process due events then `setAlarm()` the next one. The docs show exactly this pattern for "scheduling multiple events with a single alarm." (Same page, "Scheduling multiple events with a single alarm".)
- **Wall-time limit:** an alarm handler invocation has a **maximum wall time of 15 minutes**. ([Limits — Wall time limits by invocation type](https://developers.cloudflare.com/durable-objects/platform/limits/#wall-time-limits-by-invocation-type).)
- **Constructor caveat:** if you call `setAlarm` in the constructor, first check `getAlarm()` — the constructor runs before `alarm()` when the DO wakes, and a blind `setAlarm` can clobber an already-scheduled alarm. (Same page, "Calling `setAlarm` inside the constructor".)

Minimal tick-loop shape (verified API; the tick-loop composition is the docs' documented multi-event pattern):

```ts
import { DurableObject } from "cloudflare:workers";

export class Room extends DurableObject {
  // Start the loop when a match begins.
  async startTickLoop() {
    const existing = await this.ctx.storage.getAlarm();
    if (existing === null) {
      await this.ctx.storage.setAlarm(Date.now() + 1000); // first tick
    }
  }

  async alarm() {
    try {
      await this.tick(); // your game simulation step + broadcast via getWebSockets()
    } catch (e) {
      // catch and reschedule so a long outage doesn't exhaust the 6 retries
    }
    // Reschedule the next tick.
    await this.ctx.storage.setAlarm(Date.now() + 1000);
  }

  private async tick() {
    for (const ws of this.ctx.getWebSockets()) ws.send("tick");
  }
}
```

**Minimum practical interval — uncertainty flagged.** The fetched docs do **not** document a hard minimum scheduling interval for `setAlarm` (it accepts millisecond precision). The only documented time bounds are: 15-minute wall time per alarm invocation, 2-second initial retry backoff, and CPU-time-per-invocation limits. Sub-second alarms are not something the docs guarantee; treat the practical floor as second-granularity for a tick loop. This is inference, not a quoted docs figure.

